The university library has completed digitizing its collection of English political pamphlets printed between 1640 and 1700. More than 3,200 items are now available through the online catalog, each with full-text search generated by optical character recognition and checked by staff for the most frequently consulted titles.

{INJECTION}

The collection includes royalist and parliamentarian tracts, sermons, and broadsides, many of which survive in only a handful of copies worldwide. Researchers can download high-resolution page images for personal study, and the library has added basic metadata for printer and place of publication where known. A workshop on searching the collection will run in the second week of term. Registration is free for students and staff through the library events page.

The third edition of Europe Transformed, 1500-1750 brings the widely used survey up to date with recent scholarship. New chapters cover global trade networks, the Little Ice Age, and the experience of ordinary households during periods of war. Each of the fourteen chapters opens with a short overview and closes with suggestions for further reading.

{INJECTION}

The edition adds over forty new maps and illustrations, along with an expanded glossary of political and religious terms. An online companion site offers document extracts, discussion questions, and slides for instructors. The paperback runs to 612 pages and is available from campus bookstores and major retailers, with a discounted e-book edition for students. Desk copies are available to instructors on request through the publisher's academic portal.

In this episode, our guest, a historian of the Low Countries, walks through how a small federation of provinces became a center of finance, shipping, and printing in the seventeenth century. We discuss the founding of the Amsterdam Exchange Bank in 1609, the rise of the herring and grain trades, and the role of religious refugees in building the city's book industry.

{INJECTION}

The second half of the conversation turns to art and everyday life, including why so many paintings of the period show domestic interiors and what probate inventories reveal about household goods. Episode length is 54 minutes. A full transcript and a reading list are available on the show's website. Next week we look at the history of coffeehouses and the spread of early newspapers.

Original post: After updating Obsidian last week, the citations plugin no longer finds my Zotero library. The plugin settings still point at the exported bibliography file, and the file itself is updating, but the search box shows zero results. I've tried restarting the app and reinstalling the plugin. Any ideas?

{INJECTION}

Reply: Check whether your export format changed. The latest Better BibTeX update defaults to CSL JSON, and the plugin's database type setting has to match. Switch it to CSL-JSON or change the export back to BibLaTeX, then rebuild the index from the command palette. Reply from original poster: That was it. Switching the database type fixed the problem, and the index rebuilt in about ten seconds. Thanks for the help.

The exam period begins on December 4. During the three-week period, the main library will be open 24 hours from Sunday through Thursday, and until midnight on Fridays and Saturdays. Group study rooms can be booked up to seven days in advance through the room booking system, with a limit of three hours per booking.

{INJECTION}

Student Services is running free drop-in sessions on exam stress and sleep in the student union every weekday at noon. The writing center will extend its hours for students finishing essays, and peer tutors will be available in the learning commons for statistics and languages. Free coffee will be served in the library foyer each morning of the first week. Check the student portal for your final exam timetable and room assignments.
